    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/08/brooks-hyperion-elite-5-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Full-length DNA Gold PEBA feels soft and protective, with softness most noticeable in the heel; the forefoot is firmer, very stiff, and strongly rockered/high-toe-spring.",
        "Responsiveness: The ride is bouncy with strong heel energy return and quick rocker-driven roll-through, working best at tempo to long-race paces; it feels awkward and less snappy for sprinting or forefoot push-off.",
        "Stability: This is a neutral, narrow-platform shoe with minimal flare; poor midfoot stability (narrow, medially biased midfoot sidewall that collapses) plus a thin flexible heel counter and heel slippage reduce lateral/rearfoot support.",
        "Durability: Early durability is good (no major outsole wear after about 30 hard miles), foam bounce stayed consistent, and expected longevity is average-to-above-average, though rearfoot outsole patch peeling is a concern."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/07/brooks-hyperion-elite-5-multi-tester.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: DNA Gold PEBA feels plush at step-in and the heel area is well-cushioned, but overall underfoot cushioning is not maximal and the forefoot can feel firm for some runners over marathon distance.",
        "Responsiveness: The shoe is described as fast, smooth-rolling, and energetic, with solid bounce and a plate/bubble setup that adds some flex instead of a harsh rigid feel.",
        "Stability: Lockdown and lacing are excellent, and the shaped stiff-but-comfortable heel counter provides secure rearfoot hold and lateral control; however, midfoot stability was questioned for longer races.",
        "Durability: Outsole wear is not reported in this excerpt; the main longevity note is that V5 updates heel-collar materials after V4’s heel area reportedly wore down early."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/brooks-hyperion-elite-5-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The ride is notably soft/plush and squishy for a race shoe, with strong impact comfort and a forefoot-forward feel from the 8 mm drop; some testers felt it may be a bit too soft.",
        "Responsiveness: It is efficient enough to hit workout and tempo splits, but lacks the pronounced bounce, snap, and propulsive spring found in leading super shoes.",
        "Stability: Secure upper lockdown, padded heel counter, and good road control support stability, but there is no strong indication of high torsional rigidity and the platform feels comfort-biased.",
        "Durability: The thin SpeedTack outsole still showed minimal wear in testing while maintaining excellent traction, indicating better-than-expected outsole durability for the weight."
      ]
    }
